# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Planning for the State — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter One — Planning for the State'
HEAD = '''Ten passages. The opening chapter of the book, and it is not about fighting: it is about the state that does the fighting — hence the title, <i>tu guo</i>, to plan for the state. It begins with a job interview. Wu Qi comes before Marquis Wen of Wei dressed as a Confucian scholar; the marquis opens by saying “I am not fond of military affairs,” and Wu Qi contradicts him to his face — <i>why do your words go against your heart?</i> — and points at the armour in his storehouse, hides stripped and lacquered and painted and inlaid with rhinoceros horn and ivory, too hot in summer, too cold in winter, too heavy to hunt in and too showy to fight in. You say you are not fond of it; you have been preparing for it all year. This is the only narrative opening in the book and it lays the foundation of everything after it: <b>cultivate civil virtue within, put military preparation in order without</b> — both, and a state that drops either one is lost. Two counter-examples in a single breath: the lord of Chengsang, who cultivated virtue and abolished arms, and lost his state; the lord of Youhu, who trusted numbers and loved daring, and lost his altars.

Six passages of Wu Qi’s own sayings follow. Three matter most. The <b>four disharmonies</b>: between the state, the army, the ranks and the battle — fail at one level and you never reach the next, which is why “teach the hundred families and draw the ten thousand people close” comes before any mobilisation. The <b>arithmetic of victory</b>: “of the warring states of the realm, he who wins five times meets disaster, he who wins four is worn out, he who wins three becomes a hegemon, he who wins twice becomes a king, he who wins once becomes an emperor” — the more you win the worse you do, which is an odd thing to find in a military classic. And the <b>five kinds of army</b> — righteous, strong, hard, violent, contrary — each with its own counter; against a righteous army you use rites, not soldiers.

The last two passages turn to men. The <b>five picked companies</b> are five kinds of person each formed into a unit of its own: those with nerve and strength; those eager to fight so as to show their loyalty and courage; those who can scale heights and cover ground; officials who have lost their posts and want to earn merit again; men who abandoned a city or a post and want the disgrace off them. Three thousand such, says Wu Qi, can break out of any encirclement or storm any city. Note that three of the five are men with something to redeem — that ungovernable wish to make good is what he is recruiting. The chapter closes with the story of King Zhuang of Chu, who came away from court <i>troubled</i> because none of his ministers could match him: a state whose ruler has no equal is a state in danger. Marquis Wen’s son looked ashamed. From institutions to men to the ruler himself — that is one chapter of planning for the state.'''

PARA = {
 'p1-1': 'Wu Qi, in the robes of a Confucian scholar, sought an audience with Marquis Wen of Wei to speak of the springs of war. The marquis said: “I am not fond of military affairs.” (Wu Qi was a man of Zuoshi in Wey; he served Lu, then fled to Wei and served Marquis Wen and Marquis Wu, and at last fled to Chu — his life is in the <i>Records of the Historian</i>, in the joint biography with Sun Wu. The scholar’s robes are deliberate: Wu Qi had studied under Zengzi, and the clothes are a statement of standing in the Wei court. The marquis’s refusal is a courtesy and a test at once; the book opens on a sentence that is not meant.)',
 'p1-2': '''Wu Qi said: “Your servant infers the hidden from the visible and the future from the past. How is it that my lord’s words go against his heart? All the year round my lord has men flaying hides, coating them with vermilion lacquer, painting them in cinnabar and azure, inlaying them with rhinoceros horn and ivory. Worn in winter they do not warm; worn in summer they do not cool. He has long halberds made of twenty-four feet and short ones of twelve; his leather chariots have screens across the front, their wheels are wrapped and their hubs cased. To the eye they are not handsome; to drive out hunting they are not light. I do not know what my lord means to use them for. If they are made ready for advancing to attack and falling back to defend, and yet no one is sought out who can use them, it is as though a brooding hen should fly at a wildcat, or a bitch with her pups at a tiger: the will to fight is there, and death comes after it. Long ago the lord of Chengsang cultivated virtue and put away arms, and by this his state perished; the lord of Youhu trusted to numbers and loved daring, and by this he lost his altars of soil and grain. An enlightened ruler takes warning from these two: within he will cultivate the civil virtues, without he will put his military preparation in order. And so to face the enemy and not advance falls short of rightness; to mourn over the fallen falls short of benevolence.” (“The springs of war” — <i>bing ji</i>, the hinge or trigger of military affairs. Chengsang and Youhu are legendary states, one destroyed for putting arms away, the other for trusting to them — the two halves of “civil virtue” and “military preparation.” The image of the brooding hen is the hardest thing in the book. In the Kanripo transcription of the Sibu congkan text “twenty-four feet” appears as “twenty-<i>great</i>-four”; the facsimile leaf itself reads <i>zhang</i>, “ten feet,” so that is a slip of the transcription and not a reading of the edition — see the colophon. The same transcription writes “the <i>dwelling</i> of Youhu” for “the <i>lord</i> of Youhu.” The Siku text reads “to drive out <i>in</i> the hunt,” “lost his state <i>and house</i>,” and omits the connective in “to face the enemy and not advance.”)''',
 'p1-3': 'Thereupon Marquis Wen spread the mat for him with his own hands, his consort held up the cup, and Wu Qi was consecrated in the ancestral temple and made Grand General to hold the West River. He fought seventy-six great battles with the feudal lords, won sixty-four outright, and the rest he brought to a draw. He opened territory on all four sides and extended the borders by a thousand <i>li</i>. All of this was Wu Qi’s doing. (The <i>jiao</i> rite is a libation offered in the ancestral temple at a capping, a marriage, or — as here — the appointment of a commander. The West River is the commandery Wei held west of the Yellow River, facing Qin; while Wu Qi held it, Qin did not come east. “Brought to a draw” is literally “broke off evenly,” and the Siku text writes the adverb with the ordinary graph. The tally of seventy-six battles is this book’s; the <i>Records of the Historian</i> does not give it. This is the one stretch of third-person narrative in the work, and it stands immediately after the interview, as a warranty on what was said there.)',
 'p1-4': 'Wu Qi said: “In antiquity those who planned for the state always first taught the hundred families and drew the ten thousand people close to them. There are four disharmonies: when there is no harmony in the state, you cannot send out an army; when there is no harmony in the army, you cannot form the ranks; when there is no harmony in the ranks, you cannot advance to battle; when there is no harmony in the battle, you cannot decide the victory. Therefore a ruler who possesses the Way, when he means to use his people, first makes them of one mind and then undertakes the great affair. He does not trust his own private counsel: he reports to the ancestral temple, consults the great tortoise, tests the matter against the seasons of heaven, and only when the omens are favourable does he act. When the people know that their lord loves their lives and grudges their deaths to this degree, then, if he goes into danger with them, his officers will count it glory to die going forward and shame to live by going back.” (The book writes <i>chen</i> throughout where later texts write <i>zhen</i>, “battle ranks”; this is the old usage, and the colophon explains it. “The great affair” is war: “the great affairs of a state are sacrifice and soldiery,” says the <i>Zuo Commentary</i>. The four disharmonies are a chain — state, army, ranks, battle — and wherever it breaks, the next link is not worth discussing. Both witnesses lack the “always” of “always first makes them of one mind,” and the Siku text reads “makes them of one mind and <i>then</i> undertakes”; the base text construes perfectly and is kept. The Sibu congkan text reads “count it glory to die <i>to the last</i>.”)',
 'p1-5': 'Wu Qi said: “The Way is that by which one turns back to the root and returns to the beginning. Rightness is that by which one acts and achieves. Counsel is that by which one avoids harm and moves toward advantage. The essential is that by which one keeps an inheritance and holds what has been made. If conduct does not accord with the Way and action does not accord with rightness, and a man nevertheless occupies a great place and a noble station, calamity will certainly reach him. And so the sage settles the people by the Way, orders them by rightness, moves them by rites, and soothes them by benevolence. These four virtues, cultivated, bring flourishing; abandoned, they bring decay. Therefore when Tang the Completer punished Jie, the people of Xia rejoiced; when King Wu of Zhou attacked Zhou the tyrant, the men of Yin did not call it wrong. Their action followed heaven and men, and so it could be as it was.” (“The essential,” <i>yao</i>, is the pivot or gist; some read it as <i>yue</i>, restraint. The four definitions are unusually formal for this book. What matters in the last three clauses is the phrase “their action followed heaven and men” — not that the war was justified, but that the people on the receiving end did not object. That is this book’s test of a war.)',
 'p1-6': 'Wu Qi said: “Whenever you govern a state or order an army, you must teach them by the rites and urge them on by rightness, so as to give them a sense of shame. When men have a sense of shame, it is enough in the large to fight with and in the small to hold a defence with. Yet winning a victory is easy; holding on to a victory is hard. Hence the saying: ‘Of the warring states of the realm, he who wins five times meets disaster; he who wins four is worn out; he who wins three becomes a hegemon; he who wins twice becomes a king; he who wins once becomes an emperor.’ And so those who have taken the realm by many victories are few, and those who have lost it that way are many.” (“Enough in the large to fight with and in the small to hold a defence with” means one and the same sense of shame, used greatly or used modestly. The five clauses in quotation marks are a saying of the age, which Wu Qi cites as evidence. This is the most quoted passage in the book and the strangest thing in any military classic: the return on victory falls as the number of victories rises. The base text prints a vertical-form colon here, which this page has changed to the ordinary one.)',
 'p1-7': 'Wu Qi said: “There are five causes from which armies arise: first, contending for a name; second, contending for gain; third, accumulated hatred; fourth, internal disorder; fifth, famine. And there are five names they go under: first, a righteous army; second, a strong army; third, a hard army; fourth, a violent army; fifth, a contrary army. To suppress violence and rescue men from disorder is called righteous; to rely on numbers and make war is called strong; to raise troops out of anger is called hard; to cast away the rites and covet gain is called violent; to raise an affair and move the multitude when the state is in disorder and the people worn out is called contrary. Each of these five has its own way of being met: the righteous must be subdued by rites, the strong by deference, the hard by argument, the violent by deception, the contrary by expedient force.” (The first five are the occasions of war, the second five its character; the two lists do not correspond and should not be forced together. The last five clauses are the finest thing here: against a righteous army you use rites and not soldiers — grant that the other side has a case, and do not try to settle it by arms. The Siku text reads “the five have their own <i>submissions</i>” for “their own number.” The Sibu congkan text has an extra character before “accumulated hatred,” evidently intrusive.)',
 'p1-8': 'Marquis Wu asked: “I should like to hear the way of ordering the army, reckoning the men, and making the state secure.” Wu Qi replied: “The enlightened kings of antiquity were always careful of the rites between lord and servant, made the forms between high and low seemly, settled officers and people where they belonged, taught them in accordance with their customs, and selected and recruited men of quality against the unforeseen. Of old Duke Huan of Qi recruited fifty thousand fighting men and by them was hegemon over the lords; Duke Wen of Jin summoned forty thousand to form his vanguard and by them attained his purpose; Duke Mu of Qin established thirty thousand shock troops and by them subdued his neighbours. Therefore the ruler of a strong state must reckon up his people. Those among them who have nerve and strength: form them into one company. Those who are eager to advance and fight and spend themselves, so as to show their loyalty and courage: form them into one company. Those who can leap a height, cover a distance, and run fast and far: form them into one company. Officers of the king who have lost their posts and wish to earn merit before their lord: form them into one company. Those who abandoned a city or deserted a post and wish to wipe out the disgrace: form them into one company. These five are the tempered edge of an army. With three thousand such men you may break out from within any encirclement, or break in from without and take any city.” (“Reckoning the men” is counting and sorting one’s own population. “Shock troops” are literally “men who break the ranks,” a storming corps. Three of the five categories are men with something against their name — the cashiered, the deserters, those with a disgrace to erase — and it is precisely their unwillingness to be left there that Wu Qi is after. Both witnesses read “eager <i>by</i> advancing and fighting”; the base text’s particle gives the same sense and is kept. The Siku text writes Duke Mu’s name with the usual graph.)',
 'p1-9': 'Marquis Wu asked: “I should like to hear how the ranks may be made certainly firm, the defence certainly secure, and the battle certainly won.” Wu Qi replied: “You may see it at once, let alone hear it. If my lord can put the worthy above and the unworthy below, the ranks are already firm. If the people are settled in their fields and houses and attached to their officials, the defence is already secure. If the hundred families all say that our ruler is in the right and the neighbouring state in the wrong, the battle is already won.” (“You may see it at once” — the thing can be shown, not merely described. This passage takes three military questions — ranks, defence, battle — and answers each with a matter of civil government: appointments, property, and where the people think the right lies. It is the shortest exchange in the book and the plainest gloss on its title.)',
 'p1-10': 'Marquis Wu once deliberated on an affair, and none of his ministers could match him; he came away from court with a pleased look on his face. Wu Qi stepped forward and said: “Long ago King Zhuang of Chu deliberated on an affair, and none of his ministers could match him; he came away from court with a troubled look on his face. The Duke of Shen asked: ‘My lord looks troubled. Why is that?’ He said: ‘I have heard that the age is never without sages and the state never lacks worthy men. He who can find a teacher becomes king; he who can find a friend becomes hegemon. Now I am without talent, and none of my ministers can match me. Chu, I fear, is in danger.’ This is what troubled King Zhuang of Chu, and my lord is pleased by it. Your servant is privately afraid.” At this Marquis Wu looked ashamed. (“The Duke of Shen” is the magistrate of Shen county in Chu; some identify him with Shen Shushi. This is not tactics but remonstrance. The chapter began with “cultivate civil virtue within, order military preparation without,” and it ends on the ruler’s own complacency: planning for the state runs all the way up to the man at the top. The Siku text reads “Wu Qi <i>answered</i>” for “stepped forward and said,” and inverts “came away from court”; the Sibu congkan text reads “<i>withdrew</i> from court” and drops one “can” from “he who can find a teacher.”)',
}
